1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
They're adult playgrounds on the open seas,

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
floating cities bedecked with the opulent trappings of royalty.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Mechanical tribes stretching the technology of the 20th century,

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
sometimes with disastrous results. Stories of adventure,

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
daring, disaster, and dining.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Oceanliners, now on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:51,560
Since the dawn of time, people have sailed the seas,

8
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:54,460
a noble and daring enterprise with challenges

9
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:58,460
to test the stern resolve of all who would explore new worlds.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,600
But by the mid-1800s, life on the high seas

11
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:07,100
experienced a dynamic shift when the age of reason embraced

12
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:11,540
one unreasonable desire, travel for the sheer pleasure

13
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:13,440
of the traveling.

14
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:18,580
It was called deep sea cruising, and it adopted a wholly new

15
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:23,460
and elite aesthetic to make the journeys of life more important

16
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:25,560
than the destinations.

17
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,140
Certainly, passengers had places to go.

18
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:34,680
But now, 30-day jaunts across the Atlantic

19
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:37,100
could be elegant outings.

20
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:40,340
What's more, the idea was considered healthful.

21
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:43,040
In the 1880s, the British Medical Journal declared

22
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:44,840
sea voyages curative.

23
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:50,260
It was the steam-powered mechanical revolution

24
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:53,320
which allowed this evolution to take place

25
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:55,100
and permitted a ship's labor force

26
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,220
to focus on the service of a passenger's comfort,

27
00:01:58,221 --> 00:02:01,660
rather than just the navigation of the vessel.

28
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:03,820
Still, there was something unsettling

29
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:05,760
about the dimensions of the Enterprise.

30
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:18,280
Something weighing tens of thousands of tons,

31
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:21,180
yet able to float gently on the surface of the water

32
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,280
seemed a miracle.

33
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:26,020
It was possible because of an elementary principle

34
00:02:26,021 --> 00:02:27,260
of physics.

35
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:29,520
Air trapped inside the form of a vessel

36
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:32,920
creates a powerful buoyancy in relation to the denser water

37
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:34,320
environment surrounding it.

38
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,700
The industrial world gave birth to the great steamships which

39
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:44,540
united the continents.

40
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:46,600
The promise of a mechanical universe

41
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:50,320
services a grand hedonistic imperative.

42
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:53,620
Life aboard while going abroad was the refinement

43
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:55,220
of civilization.

44
00:02:55,221 --> 00:02:57,920
The experience elevated the notion of culture

45
00:02:58,020 --> 00:02:59,800
and drew the sharpest distinctions

46
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,900
ever between people and the natural world,

47
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,460
who could resist the allure of luxury.

48
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:13,980
By the early 1900s, great steamships commanded the waves,

49
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:17,640
their mighty bows slicing through hardship and rough seas,

50
00:03:17,740 --> 00:03:20,980
their names projecting the domineering spirit of well-funded

51
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:23,920
adventure with only the daunting risk of missing

52
00:03:23,921 --> 00:03:26,420
a gourmet meal standing in the passenger's way.

53
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:32,560
The Olympic and her sister ship, the Titanic.

54
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:35,340
Not all the vessels proved to be as invulnerable

55
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:37,600
as their names implied.

56
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:41,240
It was inevitable there would be calamity at sea,

57
00:03:41,340 --> 00:03:45,080
as faith in the machine age outpaced concerns for safety.

58
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:51,720
April 10, 1912, the Titanic leaves

59
00:03:51,721 --> 00:03:55,720
on her maiden voyage across the North Atlantic to America.

60
00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:58,820
In these rare motion pictures, a confident captain and crew

61
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:04,000
welcomed 1,270 festive travelers aboard the $10 million

62
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:07,700
luxury ocean liner, heralded as the safest ship afloat.

63
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:13,000
Within days, the Titanic would collide

64
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:16,480
with an enormous iceberg, and more than 1,300 lives

65
00:04:16,580 --> 00:04:19,880
would be lost as the ship dragged its human cargo

66
00:04:19,881 --> 00:04:24,420
to a grave two miles beneath the surface of the water.

67
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:26,460
The ship was believed to be so impervious

68
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:28,360
that the lifeboat supply could accommodate

69
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:31,820
fewer than half the passengers and none of the crew.

70
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:38,300
It was a tragic miscalculation, and it would not be the last.

71
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:41,260
Some of the worst maritime disasters of the 20th century

72
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:44,740
have played out in the elegant confines of luxury liners.

73
00:04:44,741 --> 00:04:51,740
The luxury liner Moro Castle, a blazing tomb adrift on the Atlantic.

74
00:04:51,741 --> 00:04:57,740
Rescue ships hover around, striving to save survivors still to be seen in helpless lifeboats.

75
00:04:57,741 --> 00:05:03,740
The vessel left Cuba with 558 on board, 318 of them happy, pleasure-loving tourists

76
00:05:03,741 --> 00:05:07,740
found themselves trapped in their cabins. Some leaped through portholes.

77
00:05:07,741 --> 00:05:13,740
The drama at its climax, filmed by an amateur cameraman among the passengers, old and young.

78
00:05:13,741 --> 00:05:18,220
Old and young, exhausted, hysterical, many unconscious, many badly burned.

79
00:05:20,060 --> 00:05:28,120
On July 25, 1956, the luxury line of the Andrea Doria and the Swedish line of the Stockholm collided at sea.

80
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:32,160
Badly wounded, the Doria sent out an SOS to the Ile de France.

81
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:36,900
But rescue procedures were confused, and 51 people lost their lives.

82
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:40,600
As rescue ships arrived, friends and relatives packed the dock.

83
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:43,080
Returning survivors were still stunned.

84
00:05:43,740 --> 00:05:46,980
I don't think anybody could go through anything so appalling.

85
00:05:47,540 --> 00:05:50,240
I don't think people in this century could visualize

86
00:05:50,241 --> 00:05:51,580
that this could ever happen.

87
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:54,460
Is this the civilization we have worked for?

88
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:55,600
That's all I can think.

89
00:05:55,900 --> 00:05:58,420
I've been through the war, but if this still happens at the Shikrep,

90
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:00,400
Shikrep are the luxury ship.

91
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:06,240
The following day, the luxurious Andrea Doria sank.

92
00:06:06,241 --> 00:06:11,240
With each disaster came new technical innovations

93
00:06:11,340 --> 00:06:14,980
and safety practices designed to protect passenger and crew

94
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:18,460
on the high seas, including mandatory introduction

95
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:20,860
of ship to shore radio, minimum lifeboat space,

96
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:24,660
and emergency safety drills on every sailing.

97
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:26,900
But none of the inevitable tragedies

98
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,700
could dim the appeal of the ocean liners.

99
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:32,200
These giant ships were expressions

100
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:34,740
of national pride and the world.

101
00:06:34,741 --> 00:06:38,440
of national pride as the American and European communities

102
00:06:38,540 --> 00:06:42,340
vied to build the fastest, largest, most luxurious

103
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:43,540
ladies of the sea.

104
00:06:47,580 --> 00:06:50,020
In September 1938, Her Majesty the Queen

105
00:06:50,120 --> 00:06:53,200
had come to bestow her own name upon the towering beauty

106
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:55,900
whose graceful hull lay ready on the slipway.

107
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,260
Thousands packed the yards, climbed the jibs,

108
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:01,760
and trained to see the biggest ship in the world take the water.

109
00:07:01,761 --> 00:07:05,460
A floating city of nearly 84,000 times,

110
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:08,200
built to consolidate and secure British supremacy

111
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:11,100
on the most important of all ocean routes,

112
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:14,100
the Queen Elizabeth.

113
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:15,900
Name her Queen Elizabeth.

114
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:30,360
When she first steamed from her own port of Southampton

115
00:07:30,361 --> 00:07:33,300
at the end of August 1939, the Queen Elizabeth

116
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:36,500
was generally accepted as the definitive luxury liner,

117
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:39,540
the state of the art in technology and grace,

118
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:42,140
as she joined her sister ship, the Queen Mary,

119
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:44,500
to broaden Britain's formidable presence

120
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:47,640
on the North Atlantic.

121
00:07:47,740 --> 00:07:50,140
Together, their remarkable story reveals

122
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:53,380
that in the 20th century, the enormous size and power

123
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:56,480
of the steamship would take on other, more dramatic

124
00:07:56,481 --> 00:08:01,020
responsibilities in volatile world events.

125
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:04,280
The Andrea Doria sank in 1956.

126
00:08:04,380 --> 00:08:07,120
In 1981, the ship's safe was recovered,

127
00:08:07,220 --> 00:08:10,320
containing over a million dollars in cash and jewels.

128
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:16,300
The year was 1931, a little more than a decade

129
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:19,900
after a World War had devastated the European continent.

130
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,480
But in the British shipbuilding community of Clydebank,

131
00:08:22,580 --> 00:08:24,280
men were working with a new vigor.

132
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:30,380
There, surrounded by a forest of derricks,

133
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:35,220
stood the mammoth keel plates of ship number 534,

134
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:40,120
an unnamed Goliath designed by the Cunard steamship lines

135
00:08:40,220 --> 00:08:43,960
to become the world's largest and most luxurious passenger

136
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:45,060
liner.

137
00:08:45,160 --> 00:08:47,100
With this bold new venture, Britain

138
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:50,700
intended to eclipse the fame of Italy's wrecks and France's

139
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:54,640
Normandy, and once again claimed the mantle of masters

140
00:08:54,641 --> 00:08:59,640
of masters of the sea with the fastest ship afloat.

141
00:08:59,740 --> 00:09:03,680
Ship number 534, the name itself suggested some mystery

142
00:09:03,780 --> 00:09:06,880
and excitement, for this would be the first liner in history

143
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:09,520
to bear the name of a living member of the British royal

144
00:09:09,620 --> 00:09:11,400
family.

145
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:13,520
Eventually, the world would know this ship

146
00:09:13,620 --> 00:09:16,400
as the celebrated Queen Mary.

147
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:19,840
But for now, it was a carefully guarded secret,

148
00:09:19,940 --> 00:09:24,140
an undertaking of great national honor.

149
00:09:24,141 --> 00:09:26,780
But the economic depression which had gripped America

150
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:30,040
in an unrelenting stranglehold was becoming a worldwide

151
00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:33,780
dilemma, extending its grasp to the British Isles.

152
00:09:33,880 --> 00:09:36,580
Only months into the Project Clydebank shipyards

153
00:09:36,680 --> 00:09:38,680
fell silent, work abandoned.

154
00:09:41,320 --> 00:09:44,100
3,000 of Britain's most highly skilled workers

155
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:46,700
joined the growing armies of the unemployed.

156
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:50,800
And the unfinished skeleton of Ship 534 took silent watch

157
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:54,140
over a nation's damaged pride.

158
00:09:54,141 --> 00:09:59,440
The hunger and desperation gave rise to forlorn times.

159
00:09:59,540 --> 00:10:04,780
By Christmas Eve, 1932, the community rumbled with misery

160
00:10:04,880 --> 00:10:06,380
and discontent.

161
00:10:06,480 --> 00:10:09,980
But the nation understood that it would take a powerful emotional

162
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:12,800
boost to ignite the spirit of the country.

163
00:10:17,860 --> 00:10:20,440
After more than two years of idle melancholy,

164
00:10:20,540 --> 00:10:23,140
Clydebank workers were called on to finish the job

165
00:10:23,141 --> 00:10:25,780
to finish the job of building the Queen Mary,

166
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:28,280
and in the process, rebuild British resolve.

167
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:34,760
So Clydebank began to live again,

168
00:10:34,860 --> 00:10:37,680
as 1,000 men marched back to work in their beloved shipyards.

169
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:47,100
The clamor of hammers, the hiss of steam,

170
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:50,960
the shrill of the sirens, all the jangle and orderly turmoil

171
00:10:50,961 --> 00:10:53,360
of the shipwrights leapt into life again.

172
00:10:53,460 --> 00:10:57,260
And from 534, lifeblood began circulating

173
00:10:57,360 --> 00:10:59,400
to coal mines, to steel mills.

174
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:03,540
534 was a building again, lost time to be made good,

175
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:06,480
three years of delay and suspense to catch up with.

176
00:11:06,580 --> 00:11:09,340
This time, 534 would be completed.

177
00:11:16,580 --> 00:11:18,720
Less than six months after resuming work,

178
00:11:18,721 --> 00:11:20,620
Clydebank was ready to celebrate.

179
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:30,300
September 26, 1934, thousands of damp but undaunted spectators

180
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:34,340
gather for the singular occasion as Her Majesty Queen Mary

181
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:37,900
prepares to christen ship 534.

182
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,940
534, the first liner in history to bear the name

183
00:11:41,040 --> 00:11:46,640
of a living member of the royal family, was poised and ready.

184
00:11:46,641 --> 00:11:51,220
I am happy to name this ship the Queen Mary.

185
00:11:51,320 --> 00:11:55,720
I wish to accept you all to stay in heaven.

186
00:11:55,820 --> 00:11:58,580
With the firm press of a royal finger,

187
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:00,960
the signal is given to launch.

188
00:12:01,060 --> 00:12:03,860
Like the British spirit itself, the massive hull of the ship

189
00:12:03,960 --> 00:12:07,820
begins to move with stubborn determination,

190
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:11,100
groaning toward the sea with greater and greater speed,

191
00:12:11,200 --> 00:12:16,640
impatient to weather the tides of magnificent oceans.

192
00:12:16,641 --> 00:12:21,240
Still, there would be another two years of strenuous work

193
00:12:21,340 --> 00:12:23,940
to prepare the lady for her first voyage,

194
00:12:24,040 --> 00:12:28,180
with more than 4,000 men required to make her operational

195
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:31,260
and to complete the luxurious outfitting which would define

196
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:32,960
her majestic character.

197
00:12:37,020 --> 00:12:38,700
The construction of an ocean liner

198
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:41,060
is performed in two major stages.

199
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:44,540
First, the hull's gigantic, and the hull's

200
00:12:44,541 --> 00:12:47,240
First, the hull's gigantic metal superstructure

201
00:12:47,340 --> 00:12:49,780
is assembled on dry land.

202
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:51,780
Enormous ribs and tertiary supports

203
00:12:51,880 --> 00:12:55,220
create the skeleton to be wrapped in metal skin,

204
00:12:55,320 --> 00:12:59,560
creating the vessel's aqua dynamic form.

205
00:12:59,660 --> 00:13:02,560
Massive exterior supports keep the upper upright

206
00:13:02,660 --> 00:13:05,100
until the form is secure and watertight.

207
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:07,260
Then this frame is moved into the water

208
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:09,760
for the completion of the interior and upper level

209
00:13:09,860 --> 00:13:10,560
construction.

210
00:13:10,561 --> 00:13:13,560
construction.

211
00:13:13,660 --> 00:13:17,440
The Queen Mary's frame was 1,020 feet long,

212
00:13:17,540 --> 00:13:22,600
longer than six Statues of Liberty stretched end to end,

213
00:13:22,700 --> 00:13:26,940
the propellers 20 feet wide.

214
00:13:27,040 --> 00:13:31,780
The rudder, as big as a house, weighed 198 tons.

215
00:13:31,880 --> 00:13:35,020
The engines generated 50,000 horsepower,

216
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:38,080
equivalent to 400 automobiles.

217
00:13:38,081 --> 00:13:41,020
But her profile hid a surprising secret.

218
00:13:41,021 --> 00:13:43,680
Part of the third funnel was a dummy structure used

219
00:13:43,681 --> 00:13:47,160
to store extra deck chairs.

220
00:13:47,161 --> 00:13:49,160
The process of appointing the Queen Mary

221
00:13:49,161 --> 00:13:54,420
spanned 24 months, an eternity for the 2079 travelers who

222
00:13:54,421 --> 00:13:56,900
had booked their passage years earlier

223
00:13:56,901 --> 00:14:00,240
and who crowded gangways for the maiden voyage to New York.

224
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,740
The Queen's career as a luxury ship was to be short-lived.

225
00:14:06,741 --> 00:14:10,640
Between 1936 and 39, she plied the North Atlantic

226
00:14:10,740 --> 00:14:14,780
with a calm dignity that belied the ugly rumblings coursing

227
00:14:14,880 --> 00:14:17,380
through Europe.

228
00:14:17,480 --> 00:14:20,220
With the hot breath of war already on our necks,

229
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:22,520
at the end of August, the Queen Mary left Southampton

230
00:14:22,620 --> 00:14:27,560
with the record number of 2,332 passengers.

231
00:14:27,660 --> 00:14:29,860
And as she made for the new world,

232
00:14:29,960 --> 00:14:32,740
she looked back over her shoulder, uneasily.

233
00:14:36,740 --> 00:14:40,780
Four days out from Southampton, the ship's radio never silent.

234
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:43,280
She heard the news the world now knew.

235
00:14:43,380 --> 00:14:45,180
No further postponement possible.

236
00:14:45,280 --> 00:14:46,980
The hour had struck.

237
00:14:47,080 --> 00:14:50,580
The parlays, the negotiations, the appeasements and promises,

238
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:52,480
all to the winds.

239
00:14:52,580 --> 00:14:55,500
Britain was at war, unready but determined

240
00:14:55,600 --> 00:14:57,100
and irrevocably at war.

241
00:15:01,040 --> 00:15:04,240
These were treacherous times on the North Atlantic.

242
00:15:04,241 --> 00:15:07,840
The Nazi strategy was to sink vessels of any enemy country,

243
00:15:07,940 --> 00:15:11,040
whether they were military ships or civilian transport.

244
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:20,620
Meanwhile, the Queen Mary's sister ship, the Queen Elizabeth,

245
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:23,000
sat at Clydebank christened but still

246
00:15:23,100 --> 00:15:24,960
to be outfitted for service.

247
00:15:25,060 --> 00:15:27,360
Her vulnerable position and significance

248
00:15:27,460 --> 00:15:29,540
to the British national pride made

249
00:15:29,640 --> 00:15:31,900
her a sitting target for Nazi saboteurs.

250
00:15:34,240 --> 00:15:37,040
To thwart any such plans, the shipbuilders

251
00:15:37,140 --> 00:15:39,340
employed an ingenious deception.

252
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:42,020
While the Queen Elizabeth waited in Clydebank,

253
00:15:42,120 --> 00:15:46,360
the shipyards of Southampton were prepared for her arrival.

254
00:15:46,460 --> 00:15:48,860
Word was leaked that she would be towed there under cover

255
00:15:48,960 --> 00:15:51,760
of night to elude Nazi patrols.

256
00:15:51,860 --> 00:15:54,660
In reality, she was sailed in the opposite direction

257
00:15:54,760 --> 00:15:57,800
on a wild zigzag course across the North Atlantic

258
00:15:57,900 --> 00:16:01,360
toward New York, stealing past enemy U-boats.

259
00:16:04,240 --> 00:16:07,480
On March 7, 1940, a partially finished Elizabeth,

260
00:16:07,580 --> 00:16:10,580
her secret and hazardous maiden voyage behind her,

261
00:16:10,680 --> 00:16:12,580
found the safety of neutral waters

262
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:14,380
and rendezvoused with the Queen Mary

263
00:16:14,480 --> 00:16:17,780
to complete her outfitting.

264
00:16:17,880 --> 00:16:20,720
But at the port of Manhattan, she wasn't equipped

265
00:16:20,820 --> 00:16:23,120
for luxury cruises.

266
00:16:23,220 --> 00:16:25,920
Both ships were prepared for war to become

267
00:16:26,020 --> 00:16:31,360
the largest, fastest troop carriers the world had ever seen.

268
00:16:31,460 --> 00:16:33,800
Throughout the summer of 1941, the Queen's

269
00:16:33,801 --> 00:16:35,040
without intermission.

270
00:16:35,140 --> 00:16:37,300
Their massive hulls and their enormous decks,

271
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:41,200
cocky throng, became familiar sights at Sydney and Fremantle,

272
00:16:41,300 --> 00:16:43,340
Trincomalee and Suez.

273
00:16:43,440 --> 00:16:47,080
By the end of the year, they had carried over 80,000 troops,

274
00:16:47,180 --> 00:16:51,480
most of them to reinforce our strength in the Middle East.

275
00:16:51,580 --> 00:16:54,980
But even that Herculean effort was not enough.

276
00:16:55,080 --> 00:16:57,520
With the attack on Pearl Harbor and the entry of the United

277
00:16:57,620 --> 00:17:01,560
States into the World War, the ships were once again retooled,

278
00:17:01,561 --> 00:17:05,100
this time to carry 16,000 fighting men in the space

279
00:17:05,200 --> 00:17:08,900
where 2,000 holiday passengers were originally intended

280
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:09,700
to reside.

281
00:17:13,400 --> 00:17:17,080
64 transatlantic voyages between the two queens

282
00:17:17,180 --> 00:17:20,080
constituted the largest ferrying operation ever

283
00:17:20,180 --> 00:17:24,120
attempted, an operation laden with tremendous peril,

284
00:17:24,220 --> 00:17:27,060
as their great speed outperformed any military ship

285
00:17:27,160 --> 00:17:30,860
which might have escorted them through dangerous waters.

286
00:17:30,861 --> 00:17:33,800
But the risk had to be accepted as the massive dimensions

287
00:17:33,900 --> 00:17:37,200
of the vessels were critical to the war effort.

288
00:17:37,300 --> 00:17:39,100
The two ships that carried 1 and 1

289
00:17:39,200 --> 00:17:42,100
quarter million men across the infested oceans of the world

290
00:17:42,200 --> 00:17:46,000
lost not one man through enemy action.

291
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:48,520
Half a million Americans know the security

292
00:17:48,620 --> 00:17:50,920
that the Queen's unescorted speed could bring

293
00:17:51,020 --> 00:17:53,320
to their Atlantic crossing.

294
00:17:53,420 --> 00:17:56,420
The unconcern of these ocean-going GI's

295
00:17:56,520 --> 00:17:59,460
can be measured by their reading, their poker,

296
00:17:59,461 --> 00:18:01,860
And their music left-handed at that.

297
00:18:08,060 --> 00:18:10,880
Even with all the manpower that the Queen Mary and Queen

298
00:18:10,980 --> 00:18:13,040
Elizabeth could muster to the front lines,

299
00:18:13,140 --> 00:18:18,180
the world held its breath on that fateful day in June 1944.

300
00:18:18,280 --> 00:18:21,780
People of Western Europe, a landing

301
00:18:21,880 --> 00:18:24,080
was made this morning on the coast of France

302
00:18:24,180 --> 00:18:27,680
by troops of the Allied Expeditionary Force.

303
00:18:27,681 --> 00:18:32,680
This landing is part of the concerted United Nations plan for the liberation of Europe.

304
00:18:33,340 --> 00:18:35,560
The hour of your liberation is approaching.

305
00:18:53,780 --> 00:18:58,940
Less than a year after D-Day, Europe was suddenly and joyously released from almost

306
00:18:58,941 --> 00:19:00,440
six years of war.

307
00:19:00,540 --> 00:19:05,480
And on the appointed day, we celebrated.

308
00:19:05,580 --> 00:19:08,480
V-E Day, when the echo of gunfire

309
00:19:08,580 --> 00:19:11,880
was transformed into a grateful and triumphant celebration

310
00:19:11,980 --> 00:19:13,580
of the free world.

311
00:19:13,680 --> 00:19:17,400
Joy beyond measure from people who fought for years

312
00:19:17,500 --> 00:19:20,300
without the guarantee that freedom would prevail.

313
00:19:23,340 --> 00:19:25,700
During the war, each of the two Great Liners

314
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:28,440
had transported nearly a million men

315
00:19:28,441 --> 00:19:31,440
and steamed nearly half a million miles.

316
00:19:33,440 --> 00:19:36,440
Now these men must be repatriated.

317
00:19:36,441 --> 00:19:38,440
Most of the miles re-traversed.

318
00:19:38,441 --> 00:19:43,440
The North Atlantic again, but the passengers westbound, homeward.

319
00:19:43,441 --> 00:19:47,440
Now no huddling below deck in blacked out quarters,

320
00:19:47,441 --> 00:19:50,440
no lurking submarine to cheat, no zigzagging,

321
00:19:50,441 --> 00:19:54,440
but direct home goings for 15,000 at a time.

322
00:19:54,441 --> 00:20:00,120
Those years of secrecy and haste, of crowded turmoil, those years are past.

323
00:20:00,840 --> 00:20:05,600
The queens are stripped of their smirching gray and gleam afresh in all their gallant livery.

324
00:20:06,160 --> 00:20:09,780
Their finery restored, they sail now as their designers meant them to,

325
00:20:10,260 --> 00:20:14,240
floating cities to bridge the seas of peace between the old and new worlds.

326
00:20:15,760 --> 00:20:21,180
The contribution of the Queen Mary and the Queen Elizabeth captured the imagination of the world.

327
00:20:21,181 --> 00:20:25,280
But nearly 30 years later, the allure of luxury ocean travel

328
00:20:25,380 --> 00:20:26,580
began to dim.

329
00:20:32,160 --> 00:20:34,920
January 1972, Hong Kong Harbor.

330
00:20:35,020 --> 00:20:38,940
The Queen Elizabeth falls victim to a ship's worst enemy.

331
00:20:39,040 --> 00:20:41,540
20 separate fires erupted simultaneously

332
00:20:41,640 --> 00:20:43,340
throughout the vessel.

333
00:20:43,440 --> 00:20:47,140
She sank where she sat, the once proud haul now nothing more

334
00:20:47,240 --> 00:20:51,180
than 83,000 tons of scrap metal.

335
00:20:51,181 --> 00:20:53,820
Strong rumors of arson were never confirmed.

336
00:20:57,220 --> 00:20:59,320
On the other hand, the Queen Mary

337
00:20:59,420 --> 00:21:04,160
enjoys a more genteel retirement and a second royal life.

338
00:21:04,260 --> 00:21:07,160
Her appointments restored to their original glory,

339
00:21:07,260 --> 00:21:10,160
she is permanently moored in Long Beach, California,

340
00:21:10,260 --> 00:21:12,100
where nearly a million people a year

341
00:21:12,200 --> 00:21:16,840
visit Her Majesty to experience the romance of a bygone era.

342
00:21:16,841 --> 00:21:21,840
The most extensive war damage to the Queens came from the soldiers

343
00:21:21,940 --> 00:21:23,340
they were carrying.

344
00:21:23,440 --> 00:21:25,940
The buildup of discarded chewing gum became so thick,

345
00:21:26,040 --> 00:21:28,280
the sale of gum was banned on board.

346
00:21:28,380 --> 00:21:30,780
Soldiers' initials carved in the ship's handrails

347
00:21:30,880 --> 00:21:35,700
forced the replacement of all the railings after the war.

348
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:43,700
1,035 feet from bow to stern, taller

349
00:21:43,701 --> 00:21:48,040
taller than a 22-story building.

350
00:21:48,140 --> 00:21:51,640
Servicing over 2,000 passengers every sailing

351
00:21:51,740 --> 00:21:56,640
with a crew of more than 900, the dimensions of this luxury

352
00:21:56,740 --> 00:21:57,640
liner are staggering.

353
00:22:00,460 --> 00:22:03,520
Slicing her path through crystal waters of the Caribbean,

354
00:22:03,620 --> 00:22:06,060
the ship's majestic profile suggests

355
00:22:06,160 --> 00:22:09,400
an extraordinary pedigree.

356
00:22:09,500 --> 00:22:11,760
Today, she is the Norway.

357
00:22:11,761 --> 00:22:16,500
though she was launched in the early 1960s as the France.

358
00:22:16,600 --> 00:22:19,040
A symbol of classic elegance designed

359
00:22:19,140 --> 00:22:22,400
to boost the battered morale of post-war France,

360
00:22:22,500 --> 00:22:27,280
she is still the longest cruise ship ever built.

361
00:22:27,380 --> 00:22:29,920
This ship was built in a town called Saint-Nazaire,

362
00:22:30,020 --> 00:22:32,680
and Saint-Nazaire was completely leveled by bombing

363
00:22:32,780 --> 00:22:33,940
during World War II.

364
00:22:34,040 --> 00:22:36,860
In fact, there was a German sub base only a few feet away

365
00:22:36,960 --> 00:22:38,880
from where this ship was actually built.

366
00:22:38,881 --> 00:22:39,581
built.

367
00:22:39,582 --> 00:22:44,680
So when this ship came up out of the ashes of post-war France, it really did represent a

368
00:22:44,681 --> 00:22:47,572
symbol that France had recovered from World War II.

369
00:22:47,573 --> 00:22:50,180
So when the ship was launched finally in 1960,

370
00:22:50,660 --> 00:22:54,692
Charles de Gaulle was there, and his wife was actually the godmother of the ship.

371
00:22:54,693 --> 00:22:55,393
She was the

372
00:22:55,394 --> 00:22:56,650
one that christened France.

373
00:22:56,651 --> 00:23:00,200
And there's a great story that after the ship had gone down the ways

374
00:23:00,201 --> 00:23:05,840
and splashed into the Loire River, that General de Gaulle came to the end of the podium, raised his

375
00:23:05,841 --> 00:23:10,120
arms to the sky and said, vive la France, vive la France.

376
00:23:10,121 --> 00:23:14,120
And then he was saying, long live France, long live the France.

377
00:23:14,121 --> 00:23:16,520
So this ship really was for them a rebirth.

378
00:23:16,521 --> 00:23:19,160
It was a rebirth of their culture, their style,

379
00:23:19,161 --> 00:23:20,280
their cuisine.

380
00:23:20,281 --> 00:23:23,060
It showed the world that France was alive and well.

381
00:23:23,061 --> 00:23:27,100
And if you wanted to, you could visit her on the North Atlantic.

382
00:23:27,101 --> 00:23:28,820
When the French built it, they built it

383
00:23:28,821 --> 00:23:32,840
in terms of 1960s standards of craftsmanship and quality.

384
00:23:32,841 --> 00:23:36,720
They lavished on this ship materials and time and design

385
00:23:36,721 --> 00:23:40,940
and energy that really is beyond the practicality

386
00:23:40,941 --> 00:23:42,560
of many new constructions.

387
00:23:42,561 --> 00:23:44,000
On her maiden voyage, she probably

388
00:23:44,001 --> 00:23:46,120
encountered one of the worst storms in her history.

389
00:23:46,121 --> 00:23:49,340
1962, February, crossing the North Atlantic,

390
00:23:49,341 --> 00:23:52,880
she hit what Captain Khoazil, the captain of the ship,

391
00:23:52,881 --> 00:23:55,220
said was the worst storm in 25 years.

392
00:23:55,221 --> 00:23:57,900
The ship went into 40-foot seas, but the passengers didn't

393
00:23:57,901 --> 00:23:59,960
seem to be too disrupted.

394
00:23:59,961 --> 00:24:01,940
It is the ship's sheer size which

395
00:24:01,941 --> 00:24:03,680
it to remain stable.

396
00:24:03,780 --> 00:24:05,420
The substantial length of the vessel

397
00:24:05,520 --> 00:24:09,180
spans the distance of more than three ocean waves,

398
00:24:09,280 --> 00:24:12,580
so that the structure is always riding the crest of several

399
00:24:12,680 --> 00:24:15,460
rises, eliminating fore and aft rocking

400
00:24:15,560 --> 00:24:17,960
under any conditions.

401
00:24:18,060 --> 00:24:20,120
Well, as of France, there were a number of passengers,

402
00:24:20,220 --> 00:24:23,400
famous celebrities that crossed between the New World

403
00:24:23,500 --> 00:24:25,400
and the Old World when the ship was used as a method

404
00:24:25,500 --> 00:24:26,600
of transportation.

405
00:24:26,700 --> 00:24:30,140
These included people like Alfred Hitchcock and Salvador

406
00:24:30,141 --> 00:24:31,640
Salvador Dali.

407
00:24:31,641 --> 00:24:33,440
But Salvador Dali was unique in the fact

408
00:24:33,441 --> 00:24:36,420
that he normally traveled with his pet ocelot.

409
00:24:36,421 --> 00:24:38,280
And the ocelot did not travel in the kennel

410
00:24:38,281 --> 00:24:40,120
with the rest of the pets of passengers.

411
00:24:40,121 --> 00:24:42,520
It, in fact, traveled with him in his cabin

412
00:24:42,521 --> 00:24:46,220
and slept in his bathroom, which gave the cabin stewards,

413
00:24:46,221 --> 00:24:50,660
after he left the ship, no easy solution to clean that cabin.

414
00:24:50,661 --> 00:24:52,260
Other travelers on the France

415
00:24:52,261 --> 00:24:55,040
traveled with more traditional pets,

416
00:24:55,041 --> 00:24:58,600
and they enjoyed extraordinary accommodations as well.

417
00:24:58,601 --> 00:25:01,540
These pets were pampered almost as much as the passengers.

418
00:25:01,541 --> 00:25:03,140
They had their own private menu that

419
00:25:03,141 --> 00:25:05,040
was posted on a daily basis.

420
00:25:05,041 --> 00:25:08,940
They also had a New York fire hydrant for the American pets

421
00:25:08,941 --> 00:25:12,080
and a Parisian lamppost for the French pets.

422
00:25:12,081 --> 00:25:15,840
So everybody lived well on the France.

423
00:25:15,841 --> 00:25:18,280
The opulence of the France attracted

424
00:25:18,281 --> 00:25:21,240
many who normally would never have considered an ocean

425
00:25:21,241 --> 00:25:22,820
voyage.

426
00:25:22,821 --> 00:25:24,920
The most famous passenger that ever traveled on the ship

427
00:25:24,921 --> 00:25:26,460
was actually not a living person at all.

428
00:25:26,461 --> 00:25:28,000
It was the Mona Lisa.

429
00:25:28,001 --> 00:25:29,340
And she had her own private cabin

430
00:25:29,341 --> 00:25:31,240
when she was being transported from the Louvre

431
00:25:31,241 --> 00:25:33,580
to an exhibition in New York.

432
00:25:33,581 --> 00:25:35,700
And security personnel actually stayed with her

433
00:25:35,701 --> 00:25:38,780
in the first class cabin the entire crossing.

434
00:25:38,781 --> 00:25:41,040
But even as early as the 1960s,

435
00:25:41,041 --> 00:25:43,740
there were rough seas for the France

436
00:25:43,741 --> 00:25:47,580
and for all of the ocean liners which crossed the Atlantic.

437
00:25:47,581 --> 00:25:50,660
The new danger wasn't from below like the icebergs,

438
00:25:50,661 --> 00:25:52,260
which claimed the Titanic.

439
00:25:52,261 --> 00:25:54,220
This time, the threat was from above.

440
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:01,940
At the time of her maiden voyage in 1962,

441
00:26:02,040 --> 00:26:07,280
the France was already obsolete as a transatlantic carrier.

442
00:26:07,380 --> 00:26:10,580
That very same year was the first year

443
00:26:10,680 --> 00:26:17,260
that was carrying more passengers across the Atlantic

444
00:26:17,360 --> 00:26:22,360
in the air than it was on the ships.

445
00:26:22,460 --> 00:26:26,400
It was easy to see the advantages of a six-hour flight

446
00:26:26,401 --> 00:26:28,800
flight versus six days at sea.

447
00:26:28,900 --> 00:26:31,880
And the drop in passenger demand had a devastating effect

448
00:26:31,980 --> 00:26:33,180
on the SS France.

449
00:26:35,680 --> 00:26:38,040
After only 12 years in service, she

450
00:26:38,140 --> 00:26:40,720
was moored at the docks of Le Havre, France,

451
00:26:40,820 --> 00:26:43,320
a purgatory for abandoned ships.

452
00:26:43,420 --> 00:26:47,780
Like so many other ocean liners, the France lacked a purpose.

453
00:26:47,880 --> 00:26:51,500
Many ocean liner companies were looking for new occupations

454
00:26:51,600 --> 00:26:52,560
for their ships.

455
00:26:52,561 --> 00:26:56,060
And cruising became a new method of vacation,

456
00:26:56,160 --> 00:26:57,960
particularly for Americans.

457
00:26:58,060 --> 00:27:00,800
In 1979, Norwegian sailing magnet

458
00:27:00,900 --> 00:27:04,240
Newt Huttstein Kloster saw an opportunity

459
00:27:04,340 --> 00:27:07,780
to adopt the France and have it become the crowning jewel

460
00:27:07,880 --> 00:27:11,380
of his family of ships, the Kloster Cruise Lines.

461
00:27:11,480 --> 00:27:15,320
She would have a new assignment and a new lease on life.

462
00:27:15,420 --> 00:27:17,860
And so the transition was a natural one.

463
00:27:17,960 --> 00:27:19,860
Ships that used to be used for crossing

464
00:27:19,861 --> 00:27:24,040
were now used for cruising and put into a new role,

465
00:27:24,140 --> 00:27:27,100
primarily, at least in the original years, in the Caribbean.

466
00:27:27,200 --> 00:27:29,000
And this ship, of course, is a perfect example

467
00:27:29,100 --> 00:27:32,180
of the transition between a ship that was used for transportation

468
00:27:32,280 --> 00:27:36,040
into one that is now used solely for pleasure cruising.

469
00:27:36,140 --> 00:27:40,120
Clauster purchased the France for only $18 million,

470
00:27:40,220 --> 00:27:42,480
the scrap value of the metal.

471
00:27:42,580 --> 00:27:46,760
But he invested over $130 million to refurbish and upgrade

472
00:27:46,761 --> 00:27:50,660
the enormous craft for Caribbean waters.

473
00:27:50,760 --> 00:27:53,060
After five years in dry dock, the France

474
00:27:53,160 --> 00:27:56,000
could no longer move under her own power.

475
00:27:56,100 --> 00:27:58,340
After being rechristened the SS Norway,

476
00:27:58,440 --> 00:28:01,340
the vessel had to be towed to the shipyards of Bramerhaven,

477
00:28:01,440 --> 00:28:05,980
Germany for 33 intensive weeks of restoration.

478
00:28:06,080 --> 00:28:08,080
Here, the Norway would be converted

479
00:28:08,180 --> 00:28:11,680
to perform less work on warm water runs.

480
00:28:11,780 --> 00:28:14,180
High speeds were no longer necessary,

481
00:28:14,181 --> 00:28:16,880
So four of the eight boilers were removed,

482
00:28:16,980 --> 00:28:19,720
two of the four propellers were eliminated,

483
00:28:19,820 --> 00:28:23,920
and two turbines were disengaged.

484
00:28:24,020 --> 00:28:27,320
Still, the engine room could generate 54,000 horsepower

485
00:28:27,420 --> 00:28:31,840
with five diesel generators pumping 25,000 kilowatts of power

486
00:28:31,940 --> 00:28:35,160
through the ship, enough power to serve as a city with a

487
00:28:35,260 --> 00:28:38,100
population of 30,000 people.

488
00:28:38,200 --> 00:28:40,940
New innovations were employed to conserve energy

489
00:28:41,040 --> 00:28:42,540
and increase efficiency.

490
00:28:42,541 --> 00:28:46,440
Incinerator and waste treatment plants on board were upgraded.

491
00:28:46,540 --> 00:28:49,540
Over 44 miles of new electrical cable

492
00:28:49,640 --> 00:28:51,980
linked the state-of-the-art remote control bridge

493
00:28:52,080 --> 00:28:55,280
navigation system to the engine room.

494
00:28:55,380 --> 00:28:57,760
And the Norway doesn't require huge tanks

495
00:28:57,860 --> 00:29:00,900
to hold potable water for the week-long cruise.

496
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:04,220
Instead, fresh water is collected from the condensation

497
00:29:04,320 --> 00:29:06,400
of the eight new air conditioning systems.

498
00:29:06,401 --> 00:29:11,400
Likewise, new navigation lades called thrusters

499
00:29:11,500 --> 00:29:12,900
were built into the hull.

500
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:16,340
There are three thrusters located in the bow of the ship

501
00:29:16,440 --> 00:29:18,140
and two in the stern.

502
00:29:18,240 --> 00:29:20,640
By pulling seawater through the openings,

503
00:29:20,740 --> 00:29:24,160
the ship can move sideways.

504
00:29:24,260 --> 00:29:30,660
To move the bow, we just simply pull this all over to the side.

505
00:29:30,760 --> 00:29:34,000
And the bow will be turning towards the porch.

506
00:29:34,001 --> 00:29:38,180
If we want to turn quicker, we turn the stern over to starboard,

507
00:29:38,181 --> 00:29:42,180
and the ship is actually able to turn on a dime.

508
00:29:42,181 --> 00:29:45,240
Steel reinforcements were welded to the hull

509
00:29:45,241 --> 00:29:48,740
to offset the 125 tons of pressure created

510
00:29:48,741 --> 00:29:50,920
by these side thrusters.

511
00:29:53,060 --> 00:29:57,660
In all 1,200 drawings and 600 tons of structural revisions

512
00:29:57,661 --> 00:30:00,800
were employed in the effort.

513
00:30:00,801 --> 00:30:04,300
Other modern amenities were also part of the refurbishing,

514
00:30:04,400 --> 00:30:08,840
including a fully operational stage theater, a disco,

515
00:30:08,940 --> 00:30:14,540
and two outdoor swimming pools, 431,000 square feet of carpeting

516
00:30:14,640 --> 00:30:17,560
and over 27,000 gallons of paint,

517
00:30:17,660 --> 00:30:21,960
enough to cover nearly 1,000 residential homes.

518
00:30:22,060 --> 00:30:27,560
The final result is indeed a self-sufficient city on the sea.

519
00:30:30,800 --> 00:30:35,100
It is a staggering achievement of incomparable dimension.

520
00:30:35,200 --> 00:30:40,940
12 elevators, two swimming pools, six bars, a Roman spa,

521
00:30:41,040 --> 00:30:43,980
a fitness center, 10 passenger decks,

522
00:30:44,080 --> 00:30:48,960
and acres of storage areas scattered throughout the vessel.

523
00:30:49,060 --> 00:30:51,520
The metamorphosis of the France to the Norway

524
00:30:51,620 --> 00:30:53,920
and the shipyards of Germany represented

525
00:30:54,020 --> 00:30:56,120
an international effort.

526
00:30:56,220 --> 00:31:00,000
When she finally made her premier run in the spring of 1980,

527
00:31:00,001 --> 00:31:03,800
This tradition continued with a crew of more than 900 people

528
00:31:03,900 --> 00:31:07,480
from 50 different nations employed in the operation.

529
00:31:07,580 --> 00:31:10,580
The official language of the ship is English.

530
00:31:10,680 --> 00:31:12,940
And everybody who joins the ship,

531
00:31:13,040 --> 00:31:14,140
they know how to speak English.

532
00:31:14,240 --> 00:31:20,020
And 52 nationalities in a close working ship like this,

533
00:31:20,120 --> 00:31:24,120
it's quite amazing how smooth everything goes together.

534
00:31:24,220 --> 00:31:26,360
We are the only ship in the world

535
00:31:26,361 --> 00:31:29,660
which is flying the United Nations flag,

536
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:32,960
and which we are very proud of,

537
00:31:33,400 --> 00:31:34,700
being the world's biggest

538
00:31:34,701 --> 00:31:38,740
and the most beautiful piece of art

539
00:31:38,741 --> 00:31:42,620
which is ever designed to sail up on the waters

540
00:31:42,621 --> 00:31:46,560
and then flying the United Nations flag

541
00:31:46,561 --> 00:31:48,500
with so many nations working on board.

542
00:31:49,580 --> 00:31:51,920
And it works very well.

543
00:31:53,620 --> 00:31:56,280
This one magnificent vessel

544
00:31:56,281 --> 00:32:00,460
has been the namesake for two powerful Marathon nations.

545
00:32:00,560 --> 00:32:04,520
Today, she proudly boasts that twin legacy of service,

546
00:32:04,620 --> 00:32:06,420
luxury, and romance.

547
00:32:09,220 --> 00:32:11,800
Before the France went on its first world cruise,

548
00:32:11,900 --> 00:32:14,200
the captain flew to every major port

549
00:32:14,300 --> 00:32:16,740
to see if they had laundry facilities that

550
00:32:16,840 --> 00:32:20,400
could accommodate the ship's linen.

551
00:32:20,500 --> 00:32:21,700
The busiest day of the week, of course,

552
00:32:21,800 --> 00:32:23,600
is when we get back to Miami.

553
00:32:23,700 --> 00:32:25,680
We dock at about 530 in the morning.

554
00:32:25,681 --> 00:32:29,680
We offload this week will be 2,020 passengers.

555
00:32:29,681 --> 00:32:32,680
And within two and a half hours, we'll be taking on 2,300 passengers.

556
00:32:32,681 --> 00:32:35,680
And there's not a hotel in the world that turns around in two and a half hours.

557
00:32:35,681 --> 00:32:38,680
That's cleaning every single room, taking on all the food for the next cruise,

558
00:32:38,681 --> 00:32:41,680
which is approximately about $130,000 a week.

559
00:32:41,681 --> 00:32:43,680
So it's a lot of shopping.

560
00:32:43,681 --> 00:32:50,680
Simple items like flour, here we're talking about £5,400 a week in a seven-day period.

561
00:32:50,681 --> 00:32:53,680
Sugar, we're talking about £1,500 of sugar.

562
00:32:53,681 --> 00:32:57,240
beef strip loin, here we're talking about 3,000 pounds a week.

563
00:32:57,820 --> 00:33:00,520
Salmon, we're talking about 700 pounds a week.

564
00:33:01,260 --> 00:33:04,560
Eggs, we're talking about 21,000 eggs.

565
00:33:05,300 --> 00:33:08,980
So I guess the chickens are busy before we come into port.

566
00:33:09,960 --> 00:33:13,560
Food is one of the highlights of a cruise ship.

567
00:33:14,080 --> 00:33:16,740
Not only this cruise ship, but every cruise ship in the world.

568
00:33:17,500 --> 00:33:19,880
It's one of the main factors where it's determined

569
00:33:19,881 --> 00:33:22,160
where even passengers choose to cruise.

570
00:33:22,161 --> 00:33:24,800
They expect to be pampered, and they expect to enjoy each day

571
00:33:24,900 --> 00:33:27,940
as it goes by, different menus every single day.

572
00:33:28,040 --> 00:33:31,740
To the untrained eye, the expansive kitchens of the Norway

573
00:33:31,840 --> 00:33:34,140
seem noisy and chaotic.

574
00:33:34,240 --> 00:33:37,000
But the rattling of dishes and the barking of the cooks

575
00:33:37,100 --> 00:33:41,640
is part of an elaborate choreography of cuisine.

576
00:33:41,740 --> 00:33:45,680
We have 167 people in the galley staff.

577
00:33:45,780 --> 00:33:47,380
We have all different departments.

578
00:33:47,480 --> 00:33:50,780
We have about 40 cooks just working in this area here

579
00:33:50,781 --> 00:33:53,369
area here in the different stations.

580
00:33:53,370 --> 00:33:57,180
We have about 30 people working just the coal station

581
00:33:57,181 --> 00:33:58,017
over here.

582
00:33:58,018 --> 00:34:04,380
They prepare all the salads, coal soups, coal appetizers, everything is made

583
00:34:04,381 --> 00:34:10,888
on board, all our pates. We don't buy anything in, we just buy all raw products.

584
00:34:10,889 --> 00:34:11,620
You won't

585
00:34:11,621 --> 00:34:14,160
find in the galley here too many fry pans.

586
00:34:14,161 --> 00:34:16,700
We do have a few small ones, but these are

587
00:34:16,701 --> 00:34:19,161
actually all of our pans that we cook for.

588
00:34:19,162 --> 00:34:21,740
We cook for 2,000 people for vegetables, you

589
00:34:21,741 --> 00:34:26,820
don't need little pots. These are all tilt kettles, they heat up very quickly and we

590
00:34:26,821 --> 00:34:28,755
just produce, mass production here.

591
00:34:28,756 --> 00:34:31,740
As I say the vegetables there are cooked in 10 minutes

592
00:34:31,741 --> 00:34:37,855
for this seating of over 1,000 people. Here we are in the middle of service.

593
00:34:37,856 --> 00:34:38,740
This is for

594
00:34:38,741 --> 00:34:42,940
the Leeward dining room, this line here, so the servers are coming in and picking up for

595
00:34:42,941 --> 00:34:48,786
that dining room. They're going to serve about 600 people in here right now.

596
00:34:48,787 --> 00:34:49,940
Over here we're

597
00:34:50,220 --> 00:34:55,520
cooking steaks to order. They'll come in, order medium rare, medium or whatever and

598
00:34:55,521 --> 00:34:59,098
these guys here will be putting them together.

599
00:34:59,099 --> 00:35:02,520
This is a turkey station, fish station, beef

600
00:35:02,521 --> 00:35:04,579
station and pork station.

601
00:35:04,580 --> 00:35:09,520
So all together we probably prepare around about 9,000 meals

602
00:35:09,521 --> 00:35:14,900
Passengers may not see the operations inside the kitchen,

603
00:35:15,000 --> 00:35:17,460
but the spirited dance of the waiters

604
00:35:17,560 --> 00:35:20,840
suggests the monumental task of keeping one of the world's

605
00:35:20,940 --> 00:35:22,640
biggest restaurants afloat.

606
00:35:32,780 --> 00:35:35,440
On a modern cruise ship, computers

607
00:35:35,540 --> 00:35:37,580
enable the crew to navigate the vessel

608
00:35:37,581 --> 00:35:41,660
the vessel with an exceptional degree of precision.

609
00:35:41,760 --> 00:35:43,760
Today's ship navigation is supported

610
00:35:43,860 --> 00:35:47,420
by electronic beacons from shore and from satellites

611
00:35:47,520 --> 00:35:50,700
held in fixed orbits around the planet.

612
00:35:50,800 --> 00:35:53,360
The action needed to steer a ship such as this

613
00:35:53,460 --> 00:35:58,300
can be performed with the lightest touch of a single hand.

614
00:35:58,400 --> 00:36:01,840
If you want to move the ship in a 45-degree angle,

615
00:36:01,940 --> 00:36:04,100
you simply pull this stick, and the computer

616
00:36:04,200 --> 00:36:06,880
will calculate what power on the thrusters,

617
00:36:06,881 --> 00:36:09,120
thrusters, what power on the propellers,

618
00:36:09,121 --> 00:36:11,980
and what kind of rudder angle we need to get there.

619
00:36:11,981 --> 00:36:15,160
They compensate everything from current and all these things,

620
00:36:15,161 --> 00:36:17,800
and we move the ship up to that direction

621
00:36:17,801 --> 00:36:18,860
like a big happy family.

622
00:36:24,240 --> 00:36:26,800
While the path of the ship is controlled directly

623
00:36:26,801 --> 00:36:29,940
from the bridge, the thrust of the engines

624
00:36:29,941 --> 00:36:32,840
is managed a quarter of a mile away,

625
00:36:32,841 --> 00:36:36,540
below the waterline in the ship's engine room.

626
00:36:36,640 --> 00:36:40,140
The boilers are the heart of the ship's propulsion.

627
00:36:40,240 --> 00:36:44,080
Inside, gigantic tanks nestled in the bottom of the ship.

628
00:36:44,180 --> 00:36:47,180
In dark recesses, passengers do not see.

629
00:36:47,280 --> 00:36:51,000
Water is heated to 932 degrees, and the steam pressure

630
00:36:51,100 --> 00:36:55,800
forces the turbines to revolve and spin the propellers.

631
00:36:55,900 --> 00:36:58,840
400,000 gallons of fuel are consumed

632
00:36:58,940 --> 00:37:02,840
during the typical seven-day voyage.

633
00:37:02,841 --> 00:37:06,340
The stability of the superheated system

634
00:37:06,440 --> 00:37:08,740
demands careful treatment and maintenance.

635
00:37:08,840 --> 00:37:11,280
On this ship, the engine room crew,

636
00:37:11,380 --> 00:37:13,180
most of whom are from the Philippines,

637
00:37:13,280 --> 00:37:16,980
work long hours and spend many months at sea.

638
00:37:17,080 --> 00:37:20,600
It's exhausting, grueling work, but these men,

639
00:37:20,700 --> 00:37:23,000
known as stokers, are not the only ones

640
00:37:23,100 --> 00:37:26,100
who spend most of their time in the deep, hidden recesses

641
00:37:26,200 --> 00:37:27,500
of the ship.

642
00:37:27,600 --> 00:37:29,340
All the people that work down in the laundry,

643
00:37:29,440 --> 00:37:31,540
they are from Hong Kong, China.

644
00:37:31,541 --> 00:37:37,540
These guys, they start working from 3 o'clock in the morning way up until 5 o'clock in the afternoon.

645
00:37:37,541 --> 00:37:46,540
For a ship of this size that carry over 2,400 passengers, we wash at least twice on a daily basis,

646
00:37:46,541 --> 00:37:54,540
over 60,000 pieces of towels, and also sheets, tablecloths for the dining rooms,

647
00:37:54,541 --> 00:38:00,540
which the dining room tablecloths have changed on a daily basis also twice a day.

648
00:38:00,541 --> 00:38:04,140
So it's a very large operation that we do run down here.

649
00:38:04,141 --> 00:38:07,480
The service that we render to the passengers and boat crews

650
00:38:07,481 --> 00:38:11,280
are to render a ton of over 12,000 people.

651
00:38:11,281 --> 00:38:13,280
These people on board a ship here,

652
00:38:13,281 --> 00:38:17,560
they do work 17 months out of the year.

653
00:38:17,561 --> 00:38:20,200
So in other words, they spend more than a year and a half

654
00:38:20,201 --> 00:38:22,020
on board a ship.

655
00:38:22,021 --> 00:38:25,760
Passengers are rarely aware of the 24 hour a day effort

656
00:38:25,761 --> 00:38:29,800
of crew members who labor on behind the scenes activities.

657
00:38:29,801 --> 00:38:32,600
Press operators publish daily ships newspapers

658
00:38:32,700 --> 00:38:35,040
in each of four languages.

659
00:38:35,140 --> 00:38:38,100
Technicians constantly monitor satellite news

660
00:38:38,200 --> 00:38:40,040
and weather reports, and combined

661
00:38:40,140 --> 00:38:43,620
with current feature films and original on-ship programming,

662
00:38:43,720 --> 00:38:46,580
broadcast five channels of nonstop television

663
00:38:46,680 --> 00:38:49,420
into each cabin.

664
00:38:49,520 --> 00:38:52,020
Most cruise liners have theatrical shows.

665
00:38:56,160 --> 00:38:59,500
In fact, these floating cities go to great lengths

666
00:38:59,501 --> 00:39:02,800
to provide passengers with many of the amenities and services

667
00:39:02,900 --> 00:39:05,700
they would expect of a vacation resort on land.

668
00:39:08,640 --> 00:39:10,740
It needs to be an invisible level of service.

669
00:39:10,840 --> 00:39:13,340
But yet all of the things that go on on land

670
00:39:13,440 --> 00:39:14,880
must go on at sea.

671
00:39:14,980 --> 00:39:17,520
The laundry, the preparation of all the meals.

672
00:39:17,620 --> 00:39:18,680
The crew has to live here.

673
00:39:18,780 --> 00:39:20,520
They have to have their own life as well.

674
00:39:20,620 --> 00:39:22,060
And plus the propulsion of the ship

675
00:39:22,160 --> 00:39:23,520
and the mechanical operation of the ship.

676
00:39:23,620 --> 00:39:27,020
All these things require hundreds of crew people

677
00:39:27,021 --> 00:39:29,620
and many different types of expertise

678
00:39:29,720 --> 00:39:33,460
that passengers, just by the nature of their experience,

679
00:39:33,560 --> 00:39:36,260
don't have an opportunity to see.

680
00:39:36,360 --> 00:39:39,400
The secret is in the sophisticated coordination

681
00:39:39,500 --> 00:39:43,540
of the support system, a choreography of people and tasks

682
00:39:43,640 --> 00:39:47,540
and careful tracking of the ship's facilities.

683
00:39:47,640 --> 00:39:50,340
How much revenue is being dropped in the casino,

684
00:39:50,440 --> 00:39:52,040
how much we're spending in each individual bar.

685
00:39:52,140 --> 00:39:54,180
I can pull up each individual passenger's account

686
00:39:54,280 --> 00:39:55,740
and find out what areas they've been spending

687
00:39:55,741 --> 00:39:58,340
the whole week from the bars to the gift shops,

688
00:39:58,440 --> 00:40:02,740
to the beauty parlor, to the sauna massage area.

689
00:40:02,840 --> 00:40:05,280
Occasionally, passengers are offered a glimpse

690
00:40:05,380 --> 00:40:08,280
of what goes on below deck through cooking classes

691
00:40:08,380 --> 00:40:11,980
and poolside ice carving demonstrations.

692
00:40:12,080 --> 00:40:15,400
Here, a master artist prepares one of two dozen frozen

693
00:40:15,500 --> 00:40:18,300
sculptures, which will eventually decorate the tables

694
00:40:18,400 --> 00:40:19,800
of the midnight buffet.

695
00:40:19,900 --> 00:40:20,900
Once again, beer.

696
00:40:21,000 --> 00:40:22,000
Thank you very much.

697
00:40:22,100 --> 00:40:24,300
This cruise ship carries two tenders

698
00:40:24,301 --> 00:40:28,540
passengers in ports too small for the huge liner to enter.

699
00:40:28,640 --> 00:40:32,900
Hoisted off the bow by powerful mechanical winches,

700
00:40:33,000 --> 00:40:36,620
each of these 100-ton mini ships is

701
00:40:36,720 --> 00:40:40,620
larger than the boats Columbus employed to explore the New

702
00:40:40,720 --> 00:40:42,060
World.

703
00:40:42,160 --> 00:40:44,680
Both are complete ocean-worthy crafts

704
00:40:44,780 --> 00:40:46,080
in their own right.

705
00:40:46,180 --> 00:40:50,300
88 feet long, 21 feet wide, they each

706
00:40:50,400 --> 00:40:54,300
can carry up to 450 passengers.

707
00:40:54,301 --> 00:40:56,960
On board a modern cruise ship, not only do you experience

708
00:40:56,961 --> 00:41:00,000
a transition in terms of your physical space,

709
00:41:00,001 --> 00:41:01,980
it's also kind of an international experience.

710
00:41:02,420 --> 00:41:05,760
Not only are you traveling to faraway places in most cruises,

711
00:41:05,880 --> 00:41:08,100
but you're also experiencing people from other lands,

712
00:41:08,420 --> 00:41:10,420
other passengers from different countries,

713
00:41:10,860 --> 00:41:12,160
crew from all over the world.

714
00:41:12,240 --> 00:41:14,840
It really becomes kind of a broadening experience for us,

715
00:41:14,841 --> 00:41:17,680
not only in terms of our physical space,

716
00:41:17,760 --> 00:41:20,080
but also in terms of our relationships with other people.

717
00:41:20,081 --> 00:41:25,080
As vacationers relax in the sun, the mothership floats

718
00:41:25,180 --> 00:41:28,520
like a triumphant mirage in the distance,

719
00:41:28,620 --> 00:41:31,020
a bustling city on the seas.

720
00:41:33,620 --> 00:41:36,060
The France was too wide by one foot

721
00:41:36,160 --> 00:41:38,260
to pass through the Panama Canal.

722
00:41:38,360 --> 00:41:40,300
French President Charles de Gaulle suggested

723
00:41:40,400 --> 00:41:42,440
that the France was not too wide.

724
00:41:42,540 --> 00:41:44,240
The canal was too narrow.

725
00:41:44,241 --> 00:41:44,941
was too narrow.

726
00:41:48,840 --> 00:41:52,680
When a ship is launched, it is customary to christen its bow

727
00:41:52,681 --> 00:41:55,320
in order to invoke good luck.

728
00:41:55,321 --> 00:41:57,760
But there are other superstitions observed

729
00:41:57,761 --> 00:41:58,960
during a ship's lifetime.

730
00:42:01,920 --> 00:42:05,760
You never bring a horse down to the pier.

731
00:42:05,761 --> 00:42:07,860
You never bring a horse down to the pier.

732
00:42:07,861 --> 00:42:10,700
That means the ship will be in bad luck

733
00:42:10,701 --> 00:42:12,840
for the next couple of weeks.

734
00:42:12,841 --> 00:42:17,820
And you never empty a bucket on the wrong side of the ship.

735
00:42:17,920 --> 00:42:23,980
Whistling on the bridge is also one thing you don't do.

736
00:42:24,080 --> 00:42:26,020
You don't go around and whistle on the bridge.

737
00:42:26,120 --> 00:42:30,860
That means you are getting bad weather.

738
00:42:30,960 --> 00:42:35,600
Sailors have their own unique way of perceiving the world.

739
00:42:35,700 --> 00:42:39,060
Their stories and legends reveal a vibrant culture steeped

740
00:42:39,160 --> 00:42:41,640
in history and romance.

741
00:42:41,641 --> 00:42:45,080
That tradition resonates today on the world's largest cruise

742
00:42:45,180 --> 00:42:48,880
ship, named for the land which spawns all those hardy men

743
00:42:48,980 --> 00:42:50,980
of the sea.

744
00:42:51,080 --> 00:42:58,460
Norway is a very small nation, but 90% of the populations

745
00:42:58,560 --> 00:43:03,860
lives less than 10 miles from seashore.

746
00:43:03,960 --> 00:43:06,840
And the small village where I come from,

747
00:43:06,940 --> 00:43:08,700
everybody went to sea.

748
00:43:08,701 --> 00:43:18,560
And, you know, when you are seaman and work your way up as a captain,

749
00:43:19,820 --> 00:43:21,560
you have many possibilities.

750
00:43:22,420 --> 00:43:25,300
You could be on a boat carrier or you could be on a tanker.

751
00:43:26,540 --> 00:43:31,280
But for me, and I think for most of them, the ultimate is

752
00:43:31,281 --> 00:43:35,280
if you are a captain on the world's largest cruise ship,

753
00:43:35,281 --> 00:43:38,880
then you have reached absolutely top.

754
00:43:38,980 --> 00:43:42,480
You cannot get it any further from here.

755
00:43:42,580 --> 00:43:44,320
Passengers aboard cruise ships

756
00:43:44,420 --> 00:43:47,820
enjoy a world of luxury and adventure.

757
00:43:47,920 --> 00:43:51,500
But living full-time aboard offers the crew experiences

758
00:43:51,600 --> 00:43:53,700
available nowhere else.

759
00:43:53,800 --> 00:43:55,160
At nighttime, we have the opportunity

760
00:43:55,260 --> 00:43:58,440
to see the things people sleep, when people sleep.

761
00:43:58,540 --> 00:44:00,980
It's like about two weeks ago, we saw 100 dolphins playing

762
00:44:01,080 --> 00:44:03,700
around the bow of the ship 6 o'clock in the morning.

763
00:44:03,701 --> 00:44:05,940
Only the early risers and, of course,

764
00:44:06,040 --> 00:44:08,580
us up on the bridge here, we were able to see it.

765
00:44:08,680 --> 00:44:10,500
It's a time where you want to take the PA system

766
